            "content": [
              {
                "type": "output_text",
                "text": "The latest notable news is that NASA’s Perseverance rover has completed a “marathon” on Mars: it has driven 26.2 miles, or 42.195 km, across the Martian surface. It reached that distance on sol 1,890 of its mission, after about five years and four months of driving—less than half the time it took Opportunity to cover the same distance.【5:0†source】\n\nA Mars Reconnaissance Orbiter/HiRISE image taken June 13, 2026, shows Perseverance and its winding tracks from orbit, with the rover appearing as a tiny speck while operating west of Jezero Crater in a region nicknamed “Arbot.”【5:0†source】\n\nNASA is also still publishing fresh Perseverance imagery: the rover’s “Image of the Week” page includes Week 284, covering July 19–25, 2026, indicating the mission continues to return regular visual data from Mars.【5:2†source】\n\nRecent context: JPL reported in late 2025 that Perseverance remained in excellent shape, had traveled nearly 25 miles by then, and was expected to continue long-term exploration, including work toward a region nicknamed “Lac de Charmes” for future sample collection.【5:1†source】 Earlier in 2026, the rover also completed the first Mars drives planned entirely by AI, a milestone for more autonomous planetary exploration.【5:4†source】",
                "annotations": [
                  {
